**The turning point:** you called out that no test may take 1–2 hours, ever. That's now codified as the enforced bar (TIMEOUT_BUDGETS.md + manifest): **≤120s per test at 32 nodes, ≤20 min per full rung, never widen a budget toward a measured wall again** — the dir_reuse 60×N→90×N→140×N drift is deleted from run.sh. The remaining work is making the product fast enough to pass, and it's now being done in bounded, minutes-scale experiments instead of hour-long boards.

**Root-caused and fixed this session (each RULE-4 proven):**
1. **mmap-fault vs bast-drain ABBA deadlock** (0.11.8) — mmap_coherency went from 0/32 wedged to 32/32 in 6 seconds.
2. **All-32-node shutdown cascade** traced to one node's AIL wedge; recycle-discriminator tripwires armed (0.11.9); dir_reuse then passed 32/32 for the first time ever.
3. **120s CAW timeout vs live-but-slow holders** (0.11.10) — waits now extend only while blockers provably heartbeat (cap 480s); converted the fio board-killer into a pass.
4. **The 6ms cold-read tax**: microbenched cross-node `stat` at 6.1ms (device time 0.8ms) — every fresh file pins its creator's cached EX, so every first reader pays a full handoff. Four cheaper hypotheses (per-op durable publish, atime, lock waits, FUA volume) were tested and refuted first. Fix built as **0.11.11: write-once EX demote** (creator releases ~250ms after closing a written file; event-driven, avoiding the refuted per-stat-timer storm shape) — built clean, **not yet deployed/tested**.

**Also found:** clyde itself degrades boards (swap was 8G/8G full; the LUN lives in its page cache) — hygiene procedure documented and host health is now a pre-run gate; and the AG tenure-starvation livelock has a deadlock-safe fix design ready to implement.

**Next session picks up at:** deploy 0.11.11 to the live 8-node rig, re-run the microbench and 6-round dir_reuse A/B (expect verify to collapse), then the rm phase, the AG admission fix, and only then the full ladders across all four conditions against the new budgets.

